  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PhysDisc 1
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "While reviewing cases in your clinic, you notice patterns in how certain
    patients respond to existing treatments. You make a note of your findings and
    compile relevant patient data. Later, you ask your residents what this could
    mean for new cancer research, and one of them responds, \u201CI\u2019m pretty
    sure that information physicians gather from patients, like patterns in symptoms
    or treatment response, can help identify unmet needs in current treatments.\u201D\r\n\r\nIs
    this true or false?\r"
  explanation: When reviewing patient data, physicians may notice patterns in symptoms,
    disease progression, or responses to existing treatments that can shape drug
    discovery. These observations can point researchers toward new biological targets
    or highlight areas where current therapies fall short. By sharing this clinical
    knowledge and patient data with scientists, physicians help ensure that the earliest
    stages of discovery focus on questions that truly matter for patient care.
  correctAnswer: True
  incorrectAnswer1: False
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Physician
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Discovery
